.TH RECIPE 1 "2026-11-08" "Recipe" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
recipe \- execute Rhai package recipes through Ginux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B recipe
[options] install|remove|cleanup|isinstalled|isbuilt|isacquired recipe
.br
.B recipe
[options] list|info|hash [argument]
.br
.B recipe
[options] validate|inspect|explain|diff [arguments]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Recipe imports explicitly selected immutable inputs, runs API 2 recipes in a
configured Ginux domain and publishes named outputs after successful execution
and independent archive validation. Recipe code and payload commands never run
on the coordinator host.
.PP
Executable recipes declare const RECIPE_API = 2 and literal const NEEDS.
NEEDS owns typed input/output names, network endpoints and upper limits; callers
bind inputs and can only narrow authority. Checks return ready(ctx) or
needs_work(ctx, reason); check errors fail execution. Only ctx.state is restored,
paired with its durable filesystem snapshot. Final JSON contains published host
paths in ctx.outputs; hooks use domain paths.
.PP
OS artifact producers and user-managed software use the same format and engine.
In the LevitateOS workspace, distro/builder/os-recipes is the builder's collection
and the top-level recipes directory is the user catalog (currently without packages).
The builder composes OS images from published outputs; recab manages image updates.
User-software activation and independent updates/removal remain to be implemented.
.SH PREPARATION
Set GINUX_BIN to the actual launcher executable, GINUX_ROOTFS to an assembled
rootfs tar and GINUX_ROOT to oci:name@sha256:digest matching that tar. Set
RECIPE_WORKER_BIN when the worker cannot be resolved from the current CLI/PATH.
The launcher must support configured downstream catalog 1002 and configured-network-audit=1
and configured-denial-audit=1.
The current native launcher needs Linux x86_64 and chroot bootstrap privileges.
Run the whole coordinator with those privileges and the explicit launcher,
image and matching worker selections. Wrapping only the launcher in sudo loses
required inherited channels. Select the compatible maintained source pair in
docs/setup-linux.md. Later local consumer corrections require their recorded
final source pair; the bootstrap baseline does not certify that final release.
Build the image explicitly with recipe-image; ordinary execution consumes it.
See bootstrap/README.md for the pinned manifest producer.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B install RECIPE
Run necessary acquisition, build and installation into the owned output tree,
with custom readiness checks, and publish the named products.
This does not activate software on the running system.
.TP
.B remove RECIPE
Run removal against the owned selected target; prior publications stay immutable.
.TP
.B cleanup RECIPE [--reason TEXT]
Run optional cleanup, with reason manual by default.
Explicit remove and cleanup use matching cached dependency products and never
install them. Missing or changed products fail before the main hook, preserving
prior state and publications.
.TP
.B isinstalled|isbuilt|isacquired RECIPE
Run the actual custom readiness check. Work needed returns nonzero.
Dependencies, including build dependencies, must have matching cached products.
Their installed checks run in dependency order without acquire/build/install
hooks. Missing or changed products fail explicitly; run install separately to
prepare them. The checked products preserve the parent's installed input identity.
.TP
.B list
List declared recipe metadata without evaluating setup/helper code.
This lists available recipe files, not installed applications.
.TP
.B info RECIPE
Show loader-owned declared/inherited metadata. Dynamic metadata is unavailable.
.TP
.B validate RECIPE [--input NAME=PATH]
Check literal declarations, typed bindings, dependencies and compiled contracts
without executing setup or launching a workload.
.TP
.B inspect RECIPE
Show declared/effective authority, dependency contracts and catalog digests.
.TP
.B explain INVOCATION|RECIPE OPERATION
Explain a static rule or retained invocation audit using its saved authority.
Absent/truncated observations remain unknown. INVOCATION may be its log directory.
.TP
.B diff OLD NEW
Compare typed inputs/outputs, endpoints, raised/lowered limits and digests.
.TP
.B hash FILE
Compute SHA256, SHA512 and BLAKE3 for a local file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -r, --recipes-path DIR
Directory for name, module and dependency lookup. Defaults to RECIPE_PATH or
the per-user application-data recipe directory.
.TP
.B -b, --build-dir DIR
Logical destination identity and shared lock. Domain work stays /work/build.
The default destination is stable under the selected state root.
.TP
.B --state-dir DIR
State and diagnostic root. Defaults to XDG_STATE_HOME/recipe.
.TP
.B --input NAME=PATH
Bind a declared typed input read-only at /inputs/NAME. Required missing,
undeclared, duplicate and wrong-kind bindings fail before setup. Use DEP::NAME
for a dependency input; inputs are never implicitly inherited. A named target input seeds owned
/out on a fresh identity; retained state restores that target instead.
.TP
.B -d, --define KEY=VALUE
Recipe-specific scope constant. A host path in a define grants no input access.
.TP
.B --target-os linux --target-arch x86_64
Currently supported native execution target.
.TP
.B -j, --jobs N
Requested job count, 1 through 16, default 1. Ginux task limits still apply.
.TP
.B --force-install
Rerun installation for the main recipe.
.TP
.B --force-build
Rerun build and installation for the main recipe.
.TP
.B --force-acquire
Rerun acquisition, build and installation for the main recipe.
.TP
.B --timeout-secs N
Lower NEEDS.timeout_secs for recipe execution, including silent commands.
The declaration defaults to 3600, range 1 through 86400.
.TP
.B --memory-mib N
Lower NEEDS.memory_mib (default declaration 2048 MiB). Ginux bounds each
process virtual address space, not aggregate tree RSS.
.TP
.B --storage-max-objects N
Lower NEEDS.storage_max_objects (default declaration 16384).
.TP
.B --storage-max-bytes N
Lower the declared writable data cap within effective memory. Hardlink data counts once.
.TP
.B --network-host HOST:PORT
Select a subset of NEEDS.network; repeatable, at most eight. An absent network
declaration grants no outbound TCP. --no-network selects an empty subset. Numeric endpoints are pinned before launch; redirects also need grants.
.TP
.B --network-max-bytes N
Lower NEEDS.network_max_bytes (default declaration 8 GiB).
.PP
Explicit limit and network restrictions apply across the dependency graph.
Each dependency keeps the stricter limit and intersects its own endpoints with
the caller subset. No restriction lends another recipe an endpoint.
.TP
.B --no-persist-state
Disable state/files recovery. Managed execution and output publication still run.
.TP
.B --json-output FILE
Write final context JSON to this file. Diagnostics go to stderr.
.TP
.B --machine-events
Emit machine-readable hook events alongside stderr progress.
.SH OPTIONAL REPAIR
install --autofix explicitly enables bounded coordinator patch/retry for eligible
build/install failures. Normal execution needs no AI provider. Configuration is
XDG recipe/llm.toml; --llm-profile selects a named profile.
.PP
--autofix-attempts selects 1 through 8 attempts. --autofix-cwd,
--autofix-prompt-file and repeated --autofix-allow-path bound repair inputs.
Patches cannot rewrite readiness checks or hide failures. Changed recipe bytes
get a new invocation identity. Providers never execute inside the worker.
.SH RECOVERY AND DIAGNOSTICS
Checkpoints require durable matching filesystem/state records before ack.
Interrupted unacknowledged work is discarded; previous publications remain.
Successful failure cleanup is recoverable with its returned state and matching
files; the producer still fails without publication. Cleanup/checkpoint errors
preserve the original producer diagnostic.
Corrupt current state fails before startup. Preserve it for diagnosis and choose
an explicit new state directory to start fresh.
.PP
Per recipe/destination scope, retention keeps four recent input identities and
two complete generations each, under the destination lock. Published products
are not state-pruning candidates. Published paths retain coordinator ownership and
private export permissions. Privileged producers need appropriately privileged
consumers or explicit product installation; a selection symlink grants no access. Diagnostic files live in
state-root/logs/invocation-id: worker.log, supervisor-stderr.log, authority.json
and result.json. The receipt preserves the actual declaration, config, pinned
endpoints and compiled contract digest; completed contexts include runtime.contract and bounded runtime.network_usage and unused-grant warnings when observation is complete.
Up to 32 recent completed diagnostic directories remain; active leases protect
in-flight logs/scratch. Later invocations reclaim recognized abandoned scratch.
.PP
SIGINT/SIGTERM cancel execution and await descendant teardown without publishing
failed work. RECIPE_FRAME_TIMEOUT_SECS bounds partial-frame transport waits,
1 through 86400 seconds, default 300, independently of execution silence.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
recipe install ./tool.rhai --input source=./offline-source \
  --build-dir .artifacts/work/tool --json-output .artifacts/tool.json
recipe isinstalled ./tool.rhai --input source=./offline-source \
  --build-dir .artifacts/work/tool
.fi
.SH LIMITATIONS
Prepared tools, image libraries and required runtime operations must be present.
Network grants do not isolate virtual hosts sharing an allowed IP and port.
There is no native host execution/network fallback or live-system activation.
Raw clocks expose host realtime/monotonic time and vDSO clocks remain observable.
Configured sysinfo returns zeros, not resource accounting. CPU selection exposes
real eligible IDs, not virtual topology. Memory limits bound per-process address
space rather than aggregate process-tree RAM. Bounded poll/ppoll does not promise
general signal interruption/restart semantics. Archive timestamps have whole-second
precision. See docs/runtime-limits.md for supported variants and errors.
External AI-provider interoperability is outside the release acceptance;
controlled autofix verification establishes retry mechanics only.
.SH SEE ALSO
recipe-recipe(5), recipe-helpers(7), README.md, WRITING_RECIPES.md
